# runtime/ADDRESS_CASTS
#
# Functions that convert a pointer to an integer for reasons that never hand Sprout a word:
# address arithmetic, address ordering, and kernel tokens.  Checked by `just runtime-word-casts`
# (scripts/runtime_word_casts.py; docs/string-header-invariant-v0.md §4.2, §4.3).
#
# Rules, one `file:name` per line (file as `runtime/x.c`; a same-named static in another file is
# not covered), a `# reason` on the SAME line:
#   - A listed function must not return an integer type of 64 bits or more (`long`, `long long`,
#     `__int128`, or a typedef of one: `uintptr_t`, `size_t`, ...), and must not take a pointer to
#     one (an out-param).  Its casts therefore cannot reach a Sprout value through its return or
#     an out-param.  A by-value 64-bit parameter is fine.
#   - A name with no pointer-to-integer cast on the host platform is stale and fails the gate.
#   - A function whose cast result can be stored into a Sprout word belongs in
#     runtime/WORD_CASTS_BASELINE, not here.
#
# The AST holds one platform's #ifdef branches.  An entry may carry one tag, `darwin` or `linux`,
# after the name: it is then allowed and checked only on that host and ignored on the other, so
# a cast that appears in that function on the other host fails as not in the baseline.  An
# untagged entry is checked on every host and must have a cast on both.

runtime/sprout_runtime.c:arena_contains       # range test of an address against the arena
runtime/sprout_runtime.c:region_find          # address -> region; index arithmetic on the base
runtime/sprout_runtime.c:region_find_slow     # binary search of the region table by address
runtime/sprout_runtime.c:open_new_region      # orders the new region into the table by address
runtime/sprout_runtime.c:region_table_insert  # sorted insert by address; keeps the heap span
runtime/sprout_runtime.c:sprout_fl_entry_cmp  # qsort comparator: free-list entries by address
runtime/sprout_poll.c:poll_tag_timer linux    # epoll: sets bit 0 of a token; returns a pointer
runtime/sprout_poll.c:poll_untag linux        # epoll: reads and clears bit 0; returns a pointer
